+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole number of seconds, like
+`120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` (days), `h`
+(hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once,
+units must be given in that order, and no spaces are allowed: `2d`, `1h30m`,
+`90m`, and `1d2h3m4s` are all valid. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is
+the same as `45s`. A `timedelta` default is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Timeout: {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
